CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Function (BRDF) as a surface interaction model. An incoming light ray strikes a green surface at a point, where the surface normal n points upward and two ray vectors p and q define the local surface coordinate frame. Multiple outgoing arrows emanating in a hemisphere of directions represent the scattered radiance, and the governing equation shows that the reflected intensity depends on the incident radiance, surface orientation, wavelength, and surface material properties. The spread of outgoing rays captures the fact that real surfaces scatter light across many directions rather than reflecting it specularly into a single one. This figure motivates the BRDF as the general mathematical model that describes how a surface converts incoming irradiance into outgoing radiance across all viewing angles.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Large green parallelogram surface patch (ground plane) with thick black outline, drawn receding to the upper-right
  - Small black ellipse marking the surface hit point near the center-left of the patch
  - Thick incoming ray ℓ_in entering from the upper-left, colored with a yellow→orange→red gradient along its length, terminating in a red arrowhead at the hit point
  - Black surface-normal arrow n standing vertically up from the hit point
  - Small black right-angle square marker between the normal and the surface plane
  - Black tangent vector arrow p, lying in the surface plane, pointing back-left from the hit point
  - Black tangent vector arrow q, lying in the surface plane, pointing back-right from the hit point
  - Fan of ~10-12 light/medium green arrows of varying lengths radiating from the hit point in all directions over the hemisphere (the scattered-radiance lobe), including several that lie nearly flat along the surface
  - One prominent dark-green outgoing arrow ℓ_out, longest of the set, leaving toward the upper-right near the mirror direction
  - Label ℓ_in(λ) placed beside the incoming ray, upper-left
  - Label ℓ_out = F(ℓ_in, n, λ, p, q) placed at the tip of the dark-green outgoing arrow, upper-right
  - Bold labels p, n, q beside their respective black arrows

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: the green surface is a flat square patch in the XZ plane, x ∈ [-4.5, 4.5], z ∈ [-3.5, 3.5], y = 0, rendered as a parallelogram under the axonometric pose with a ~0.09-unit-thick black outline (#000). Surface fill bright chartreuse green #66EE00 (slightly darker #55CC00 at grazing shading). The hit point (origin of all vectors) sits at world (-0.6, 0, 0.3) — left of the patch center and slightly toward the viewer, matching the source where the vectors cluster in the left-center of the parallelogram; draw a small black ellipse of radius 0.22 × 0.10 flat on the plane there. Normal n: from hit point straight up +Y, length 1.15, black shaft radius 0.055, cone head length 0.35 radius 0.14; label 'n' in bold just above-left of the head. Right-angle marker: small black square of side 0.22 in the plane spanned by +Y and the +X direction, sitting at the foot of the normal on its right side. Tangent p: unit direction ≈ (-0.78, 0, -0.63) (back-left in world = up-left on screen), length 1.05, same black arrow styling, label 'p' beyond its tip. Tangent q: unit direction ≈ (0.80, 0, -0.60) (back-right = up-right on screen), length 1.05, label 'q' beyond its tip. p and q lie exactly in the plane (y=0) and are mutually ~90° apart about n. Incoming ray ℓ_in: a thick tube (radius 0.11) coming from world (-3.2, 3.6, 1.6) down to the hit point, i.e. propagation direction ≈ (0.52, -0.78, -0.35) normalized; default incident unit vector ω_i (pointing FROM surface TOWARD light) ≈ (-0.52, 0.78, 0.35), about 39° from the normal. Color it as a gradient along its length: yellow #FFE000 at the far end → orange #FF8A00 midway → red #FF2A00 at the arrowhead, arrowhead cone length 0.45 radius 0.17 in red. Label 'ℓ_in(λ)' floats to the right of the ray about 2/3 of the way up. Outgoing lobe: sample ~40-60 directions ω_o over the upper hemisphere (Fibonacci sphere, y>0.02), draw each as an arrow from the hit point with length L = 0.35 + 2.4·f_r(ω_i,ω_o)·(n·ω_i), where f_r = k_d/π + k_s·(m+2)/(2π)·max(0, r·ω_o)^m, r = 2(n·ω_i)n − ω_i is the mirror direction, m = 2/α² − 2 with α = max(0.02, roughness)² (so roughness 1 → nearly diffuse, roughness 0 → spike), k_d = 0.45, k_s = 0.55 for the glossy model, k_d=0.9/k_s=0 for Lambertian, k_d=0/k_s=1 for Mirror. Cull arrows shorter than 0.3 so only ~10-14 are visible at once, matching the figure's arrow count. Lobe arrows: medium green #3FA82F at 0.85 opacity, shaft radius 0.035, head 0.22 long; the single longest arrow (nearest the mirror direction) is drawn dark green #1A7A14, shaft radius 0.06, length scaled up ×1.25, and carries the label 'ℓ_out = F(ℓ_in, n, λ, p, q)' at its tip in the upper-right. Wavelength tint: multiply arrow lengths by a green-surface reflectance R(λ) = 0.12 + 0.85·exp(−((λ−545)/55)²), and tint the lobe arrows toward the spectral color of λ; surface fill hue may shift slightly with λ but stays recognizably green at the 550 default. Camera pose: standard isometric-style axonometric with the plane receding up-to-the-right, so the far edge of the patch runs across the upper third of the frame and the near corner sits near the bottom-left, as in the source.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "dragIncoming",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "The whole outgoing lobe is anchored to the incoming direction: swing ℓ_in around the hemisphere and the reflected radiance pattern (and the mirror-direction peak) swings with it, while the total reflected energy falls off as cos θ_i near grazing."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The scattered arrows form a three-dimensional lobe over the whole hemisphere, not a flat fan in the plane of incidence."
  },
  {
    "id": "roughness",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Surface roughness (specular spread)",
    "range": [
      0,
      1,
      0.01
    ],
    "default": 0.45,
    "teaches": "Roughness controls how tightly the outgoing radiance concentrates around the mirror direction — a smooth surface collapses the lobe to one arrow, a rough one spreads it into a near-uniform hemisphere."
  },
  {
    "id": "materialModel",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "BRDF model",
    "options": [
      "Lambertian (diffuse)",
      "Glossy (diffuse + specular)",
      "Mirror (specular only)"
    ],
    "default": "Glossy (diffuse + specular)",
    "teaches": "The same F(·) framework spans every material: a constant lobe, a lobe with a bright peak, and a single spike are all just different choices of the reflectance function."
  },
  {
    "id": "wavelength",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Wavelength λ (nm)",
    "range": [
      400,
      700,
      5
    ],
    "default": 550,
    "teaches": "F depends on λ as well as geometry: at the surface's absorbing wavelengths the same geometry returns much shorter arrows, which is what makes the patch look colored."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Ideal diffuse surface",
    "narration": "With a Lambertian BRDF every outgoing arrow has the same length scaled by cos θ_o — the surface forgets which way the light came from and looks equally bright from every view direction.",
    "state": {
      "materialModel": "Lambertian (diffuse)",
      "roughness": 1,
      "wavelength": 550
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Glossy surface — a lobe appears",
    "narration": "Adding a specular term grows a lobe around the mirror direction. The dark-green ℓ_out arrow is now clearly the longest, but light still scatters everywhere, exactly as in the figure.",
    "state": {
      "materialModel": "Glossy (diffuse + specular)",
      "roughness": 0.45,
      "wavelength": 550
    }
  },
